ON DATA

Code in this repository is MIT licensed. The datasets it downloads are not:
each retains its original licence and terms. See 00_setup/README.md for the
source and licence of every dataset - Iris and Flights (public domain),
Penguins (CC0), the UCI sets including Adult, Wine Quality, Bike Sharing,
Banknote, Seeds, Rice and Auto MPG (CC BY 4.0), MovieLens 100K (GroupLens
research terms), and MNIST and Fashion-MNIST (open, via Keras).

NOTE IN PARTICULAR THAT MOVIELENS 100K MAY NOT BE REDISTRIBUTED, which is
why it is downloaded rather than committed. No dataset is vendored in this
repository; every one is fetched from its publisher at run time.

Penguins and Flights are fetched through the seaborn-data mirror
(github.com/mwaskom/seaborn-data), which repackages them; their original
licences are the ones above.

ON PYTHON PACKAGES

Installed by pip, not vendored here. Each keeps its own licence.

  requirements.txt      the core stack
    numpy, pandas, scipy, scikit-learn, seaborn, statsmodels  BSD 3-Clause
    matplotlib                        Matplotlib licence (BSD-compatible)
    xgboost, requests                 Apache License 2.0
    truststore                        MIT

  requirements-dl.txt   the deep-learning and Google Cloud additions
    keras, tensorflow                 Apache License 2.0
    torch                             BSD 3-Clause
    google-cloud-aiplatform           Apache License 2.0
    google-cloud-bigquery             Apache License 2.0
    kfp (Kubeflow Pipelines SDK)      Apache License 2.0

ON GOOGLE CLOUD

No script in this repository calls a Google Cloud API or invokes `gcloud`.
The Vertex AI, BigQuery and Kubeflow material in 05_gcp is documented, and
the client libraries above are listed because they are installed, not
because anything here authenticates. Running any of it against a project of
your own is your business, under Google's terms, not this repository's.

ON WHAT THE NUMBERS ARE WORTH

Accuracies, runtimes and costs quoted in the READMEs were measured on one
laptop against the datasets named above, and are stated as such. They will
differ on yours. Nothing here is a benchmark.
